      "updated": "2026-11-07",
      "excerpt": "NavalFireVR is a naval fire safety training simulation in Unreal Engine 5. The main menu lets a trainee choose a role and a fire scenario before entering a shipboard exercise, t…",
      "body": "# Project Overview\n\nNavalFireVR is a **naval fire safety training simulation** in Unreal Engine 5. The main menu lets a trainee choose a role and a fire scenario before entering a shipboard exercise, themed on the Moroccan FREMM 701. The current expanded ship + kitchen CO2 drill is the **first playable slice**, not the full scope.\n\n## Status at a glance (2026-11-03)\n\n| Area | State |\n|---|---|\n| Main menu with role × fire-type routing | ✅ Implemented, 42 routes |\n| Kitchen CO2 drill (desktop + VR bindings) | ✅ Implemented, verified in editor |\n| Sea scenery, bow, signage, mouse capture | ✅ Installed in saved assets |\n| Classe F (cooking oil) fire behavior | ⏳ Pending — current fire is electrical |\n| Role-specific objectives per scenario | ⏳ Not started |\n| Performance profiling (Play, standalone, VR) | ⏳ Not started |\n| Packaged build / headset frame rate | ❌ Untested |\n\n## Design principles\n\n1. **Menu flow:** main menu → role → fire scenario → briefing & learning objectives → dedicated scenario level → debrief (event timeline + role-specific assessment).\n2. **Separate levels per scenario.** The same Play session already stutters, so never load every scenario in one ship map. Use a lightweight menu map, one exercise map at a time; share Blueprints/materials/data assets, but keep scenario actors, audio, fire FX, and local geometry in the scenario's own persistent level.\n3. **Compact scenario data definitions** so one fire can be played from several roles. Record objective events (alarm, doors/ventilation, power isolation, agent choice, reports, crew accountability, suppression, reignition, recovery).\n4. **No runtime Python.** Editors scripts build/save Blueprints; the game runs on saved Blueprints alone.\n5. **Separate maps reduce loaded content, but do not cure expensive shaders/effects** — profiling is still required before setting a performance budget.\n\n## Key folders\n\n| Path | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `Content/NavalFireVR/Menu_v1` | Menu map `L_NavalMenu`, menu art texture `T_FREMM_Menu` |\n| `Content/NavalFireVR/ExpandedShip/Build_001` | `L_SECUREX_Expanded` (drill map) |\n| `Content/NavalFireVR/Scenarios` | 41 route maps + shared `L_Ship_NoFire` environment |\n| `Content/NavalFireVR/Signage_v1` | Font atlases and sign materials |\n| `Content/NavalFireVR/Experience_v2` | Sea material `M_GentleSea` |\n| `Tools\\Python` | Editor build/repair scripts → [[Python Scripts Index]] |\n| `Tools\\Docs` | Guides and manifests → [[Docs Index]] |\n| `Tools\\Art\\Menu` | Menu artwork source (`fremm_menu_v1.png`) |\n| `NavalVault` | This Obsidian vault |\n\n## Related notes\n\n[[Curriculum & Sources]] · [[Menu System]] · [[Ship Environments]] · [[Fire Drill Scenario]] · [[TODO — Roadmap]]\n\n## Graphics status — 2026-11-07\n\nApplied and saved a ship-wide graphics pass on the default expanded map and shared fire-free environment. It covers all ten rooms, corridor wall panels, detailed hatch/exhaust hardware, room services and equipment material finishes. Saved reload checks passed; sign texts/positions and GameMode matched recovery copies. Final lighting and headset performance remain unverified. See [[Ship Realism — Imported Environment]].\n",
