      "updated": "2026-11-03",
      "excerpt": "All training logic must cite its lecture deck and slide number. Treat legacy training statements as source material requiring review before any claim of real-world operational a…",
      "body": "# Curriculum & Sources\n\nAll training logic must cite its **lecture deck and slide number**. Treat legacy training statements as source material requiring review before any claim of real-world operational authority.\n\n## Source material\n\n- Source folder (original): `C:\\Users\\user\\Desktop\\AJ\\3eme-moi\\SOE\\Sécurité navale`\n- Four French training decks: `secu s1-3.ppt` → `secu s4-2.ppt`, **115 slides** total\n- Slide-indexed text archive in the project: `Tools\\Docs\\NAVAL_SAFETY_SOURCE.json`\n- Original decks + 8 JPEG photos are copied to `Tools\\Docs\\NavalSafetySources`\n- ⚠️ Some diagram/image content is not captured by text extraction — consult original slides and photos for visual details\n\n## The four lectures\n\n### Lecture 1 — Fire fundamentals (slides 7–23)\nCombustion, fire triangle, fire growth, heat transfer. Fire classes:\n- **A** solid materials · **B** hydrocarbons · **C** gases · **D** metals · **F** cooking oils/fats · **HC** special/unclassified (munitions, chemicals)\nShipboard materials and the safety organization are introduced.\n\n### Lecture 2 — Command structure & response (slides 3–27)\nImmediate sequence: **alarm → attack where appropriate → confinement → report** to the intervention director (DDI) then overall director (DDL). Organic command chain detailed in [[Roles & Command Chain]].\n\n### Lecture 3 — Extinguishing methods & agents (slides 3–27)\nMethods: cooling, smothering, isolation, inhibition. Agents: water, water mist, foam, additives, steam, CO2, inhibiting agents, powder. Also protective equipment, breathing apparatus, symbology, smoke management.\n\n**Key agent facts (lecture 3):**\n- Slide 20 recap: water+additive → class A · CO2 → electrical equipment · foam → class B\n- Slides 15–16: CO2 asphyxiation risk; prohibited for class D; inadvised for class A\n- **Agent selection must be scenario-specific.** The galley exercise is an electrical appliance fire, *not* a cooking-oil fire (CO2 choice follows [FIA guidance](https://www.fia.uk.com/static/uploaded/814f0141-6e91-4181-aaeb45ea82c55910.pdf))\n\n### Lecture 4 — Prevention (slides 2–27)\nPrevention rounds, compartment risk classification, hot work permit, hazardous materials movement, dry dock, stability, watertightness, readiness states, prevention visits.\n\n## Related\n\n[[Roles & Command Chain]] · [[Fire Drill Scenario]] · [[TODO — Roadmap]]\n",
