      "excerpt": "2026-11-06",
      "body": "# Fire Equipment Lockers — MCP Repair & Readability\n\n## Date\n\n2026-11-06\n\n## Final state\n\nFour independent fire-equipment wardrobes are placed and saved in the Unreal map:\n\n`/Game/NavalFireVR/ExpandedShip/Build_001/L_SECUREX_Expanded`\n\nDestination: **LOCAL INCENDIE** (the aft starboard fire-equipment room), not PC MACHINES or SALLE DES MACHINES.\n\nWardrobe categories:\n\n- **TPB** — green\n- **TIS** — red\n- **TISA** — navy blue\n- **ARI** — petrol blue\n\nWorld centres: `(-2304,710,0)`, `(-2176,710,0)`, `(-2048,710,0)`, `(-1920,710,0)`; each wardrobe is approximately 120 × 60 × 220 cm. The room title is centred above them.\n\n## Contents and interaction structure\n\n- 4 wardrobe Actors\n- 21 independently selectable equipment Actors\n- 1 shared French title Actor\n- Equipment lists and mesh references are bound in the wardrobe Blueprints.\n- The `NavalFire_Lockers_v2` tag identifies the installed set.\n- Existing room geometry, hose racks, extinguishers and kitchen-drill logic were preserved.\n- 74 old north-wall display props were hidden and set to `NoCollision`; they were not deleted.\n\n## Readable labels correction\n\nAll 26 TextRender components were updated and saved:\n\n- 21 equipment labels\n- 4 wardrobe headings\n- 1 shared room title\n\nEquipment labels use white `RobotoDistanceField` text with the compatible `DefaultTextMaterialOpaque` material, on dark navy backing plates. Category headings keep their coloured identity bands with a navy text backing for contrast.\n\nSizing:\n\n- Equipment labels: 7.2 cm world size\n- Lowest TPB/TIS labels: 6 cm\n- Wardrobe headings: 17 cm\n- Room title: 16 cm\n\nLong French names are wrapped across lines, plates were enlarged, and bottom-row positions were raised to avoid floor clipping. The viewport was checked from approximately 3.6 m (entrance view). Headset readability has not yet been tested.\n\n## Source assets and scripts\n\n- `Tools/Python/install_fire_lockers.py` — source installer; preserves the current label choices and rebinding logic.\n- `Tools/Python/fire_locker_geometry.py` — catalogue and simple wardrobe structures.\n- `Tools/Python/fire_locker_detailed.py` — detailed procedural mesh exporter.\n- `Tools/Python/fix_fire_equipment_labels.py` — native Unreal setters used for the final label repair.\n- `Tools/Docs/RUN_FIRE_LOCKERS.md` — run guide and inventory.\n- `Tools/Docs/MCP_FIRE_LOCKERS_REPAIR.json` — backup, asset roots and actual actor references.\n- `/Game/NavalFireVR/FireEquipment/Build_20261106_003107_763973` — imported mesh/material/Blueprint asset set.\n\nFinal editor log marker:\n\n`FIRE_LABELS_READABLE_OK : 26 texts saved, 21 navy equipment plates, Roboto distance-field font`\n\n## Important recovery notes\n\n- The original installer failed during an `EquipmentMesh` reference step and cleaned up its spawned actors. The successful placement was completed through native Unreal MCP SceneTools, then saved.\n- Raw MCP property edits on Blueprint-owned components can reconstruct components and reset earlier changes. The final label repair therefore used Unreal Python native setters (`set_font`, `set_text_material`, `set_text`, `set_world_size`, `set_relative_scale3d`, `modify`, save).\n- Do not rerun the obsolete raw-property label repair scripts.\n- No Play/VR test was performed during this repair; the existing kitchen drill was left intact.\n\n## Next optional step\n\nRun a short headset/PIE check from the room entrance and at the far end of LOCAL INCENDIE. If any label still needs more distance, adjust only the native label script and preserve the navy/white contrast system.\n\n",
